seg-d7280bb554bf = <a1><b>Kshana — PNT simulator</b> on the JetBrains Marketplace</a1> — or, in any JetBrains IDE, <em>Settings → Plugins → Marketplace → search "Kshana"</em>.

# text
# c1: .toml
seg-959ab829762c = A native plugin that runs the <b>Kshana</b> PNT-resilience simulator from inside any JetBrains IDE (IntelliJ IDEA, CLion, RustRover, PyCharm, …). Right-click a scenario <c1/> → <b>Run Kshana Scenario</b>; the figures of merit and result JSON stream into the <b>Kshana</b> tool window.

# text
seg-594199d2ce06 = Complements the <a1>MCP server</a1> (which gives JetBrains <em>AI Assistant</em> access to Kshana): this plugin is the <em>human</em>, point-and-click path.

# text
# c1: .toml
# c2: kshana <file>
seg-a356f2c30afb = <b>Run Kshana Scenario</b> action on any <c1/> (editor + project-view context menus) — runs <c2/> and shows the output in the Kshana tool window.

# text
# c1: receiver-trust
# c2: .toml
# c3: kshana receiver-trust
seg-116d04ae5538 = <b>Assess Receiver Trust</b> on a <c1/> scenario <c2/> (<c3/>): the trust timeline, with a 0-100 score and reasons for a vessel. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment (IEC { "61108" }, IEC { "61162" }): the operator remains responsible for the navigation of the vessel.

# text
# c1: nmea-scenario
# c2: .toml
# c3: kshana nmea-scenario
seg-726d88d0dea7 = <b>Generate Training NMEA</b> on a <c1/> <c2/> (<c3/>): synthetic bridge NMEA and an instructor log, written next to the scenario. Text only; for training, never for a vessel's live navigation systems.

# text
# c1: .csv
seg-99510426a384 = <b>Build Interference Map</b> on an ADS-B or AIS <c1/> (project view; asks which approved dataset it is) and <b>Route Exposure</b> on a route file (asks for the folder of maps). A degraded cell does not identify interference as the cause; not a forecast.

# text
# c1: .toml
# c2: kshana bench-export
seg-8c50e6aeb5e2 = <b>Export Test-Bench Files</b> on a scenario <c1/> (<c2/>): vehicle motion, NMEA and labelled events for a laboratory GNSS simulator, written next to the scenario. It writes no signal; run a simulator only where authorised.

# text
# c1: .json
# c2: kshana compliance-report
# c3: kshana compliance-report --mapping
seg-79c553c303c5 = <b>Build Compliance Report</b> on a run result <c1/> (<c2/>) and <b>Tools → Show Compliance Mapping</b> (<c3/>): the public-framework mapping, shown with the engine's own statement word for word.

# text
# c1: kshana evidence verify
# c2: .pub
seg-9b6ce5bbb523 = <b>Verify Evidence Pack</b> on an evidence-pack folder (project view; <c1/>): asks for the signer's public key ({ "64" } hex digits or a <c2/> file; always pinned, the plugin never accepts an unpinned signer) and optionally the full session log. Only a verified result reads as verified; an intact pack with an unpinned signer reads "NOT VERIFIED".

# text
# c1: receiver-trust
# c2: .json
# c3: kshana trust-telemetry --result
seg-253d5b7f94ce = <b>Render Trust Telemetry</b> on a <c1/> result <c2/> (<c3/>): Prometheus text, CEF/LEEF syslog events or Home Assistant MQTT lines printed to the Kshana window. Nothing is sent or served; the running exporters stay on the command line.

# text
# c1: .csv
# c2: kshana imu-fit
# c3: hybrid
# c4: fusion
# c5: [accel_classical]
seg-ce12ebcdccb5 = <b>Fit Sensor Parameters from IMU Log</b> on a stationary IMU <c1/> (<c2/>): the accelerometer and gyro parameters of the <c3/> and <c4/> scenarios fitted by Allan deviation, printed to the Kshana window as an <c5/> table. Nothing is written; the fit is MODELLED.

# text
# c1: kshana --validate
# c2: kshana kinds
# c3: .toml
# c4: kshana receiver-trust live --file
seg-b81f27c51c22 = <b>Validate Scenario</b> (<c1/>), <b>List Scenario Kinds</b> (Tools menu; <c2/>) and <b>Assess Receiver Stream</b> (a vessel session <c3/> plus a stored NMEA log you choose; <c4/>, scored once with no socket and no gate).

# text
# c1: kshana example
# c2: .toml
# c3: .toml
# c4: .geojson
seg-38037d36639c = <b>Insert Example Scenario</b> (project view folder or Tools menu; <c1/>): pick a bundled scenario and it is written as a new <c2/> (never over a file). <b>Export Scenario</b> on a <c3/> (SP3, OMM, OEM, CZML, KML, GeoJSON, STK, SigMF; simulated output only), <b>Animate Scenario</b> (HTML or SVG, opened in the browser) and <b>Import Route</b> on a route <c4/> (asks which scenario).

# text
# c1: .toml
# c2: kshana receiver-trust evidence
# c3: kshana evidence keygen
# c4: kshana evidence attach-timestamp
# c5: openssl ts -verify
seg-1f1b18f8e102 = <b>Create Evidence Pack</b> on a receiver-trust session <c1/> (<c2/>): asks the window, a signing key file you already have, and the output folder. The plugin never makes, reads or stores a key (<c3/> stays on the command line). <b>Attach Timestamp</b> on a pack folder (<c4/>): Kshana does not check the timestamp authority's signature; run <c5/> yourself.

